//////////////////////// TITLE /////////////////////////////////

Self-Balancing Binary Search Tree (BST)

//////////////////////// ROTATION /////////////////////////////////

A self-balancing BST maintains a balanced structure by performing rotations when the tree becomes unbalanced. Rotations preserve the BST property:

T1 < y < T2 < x < T3

//////////////////////// RIGHT ROTATION /////////////////////////////////

Performed when the left subtree becomes heavy.

Before:
        x
       / \
      y   T3
     / \
   T1  T2

After:
        y
       / \
     T1   x
         / \
        T2  T3

Right Rotation  ----->
Left Rotation   <-----

//////////////////////// EXAMPLE /////////////////////////////////

Before Right Rotation:
          30
         /  \
       20    40
      /  \
    10    25
   /
  5

x = 30
y = 20
T1 = 5, 10
T2 = 25
T3 = 40

Left side of 30 is heavier, so a right rotation is performed.

After Right Rotation:
          20
         /  \
       10    30
      /     /  \
     5     25   40

//////////////////////// IMPORTANT POINTS /////////////////////////////////

1. Right Rotation moves the left child up.
2. Left Rotation moves the right child up.
3. Rotations do not violate the BST property.
4. Used in self-balancing trees such as AVL Tree and Red-Black Tree.
5. Purpose of rotations is to keep the tree height small.
6. A balanced tree provides efficient searching, insertion, and deletion.

//////////////////////// TIME COMPLEXITY /////////////////////////////////

Operation   Average    Worst
Search      O(log n)   O(log n)
Insertion   O(log n)   O(log n)
Deletion    O(log n)   O(log n)

//////////////////////// SUMMARY /////////////////////////////////

A right rotation changes:
        x
       /
      y

into:
      y
       \
        x

while maintaining the ordering: T1 < y < T2 < x < T3

This allows a self-balancing BST to restore balance efficiently.

//////////////////////// TYPES /////////////////////////////////

Self Balancing BST 
          |--> AVL Tree
          |--> Red Block Tree
